WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Hey, welcome to Planetarian Daily, episode 118 for July 12, 2026.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.620
Let's get into today's research and health developments.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.040
Columbia researchers link serotonin signaling

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.400
to faster progression of degenerative mitral valve disease

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.280
in patients carrying a specific genetic variant.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.980
Columbia University scientists found that patients

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.320
with degenerative mitral regurgitation

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.840
who carry a particular genetic variant

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.720
and take SSRI antidepressants show accelerated valve damage.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.920
The work identifies serotonin as a driver

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.860
that worsens structural changes in the valve tissue.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.800
Researchers examined how medication choice could influence

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.040
surgical timing in genetically susceptible individuals.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.260
The team is now examining whether altering serotonin pathways

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.680
might slow progression in this subgroup.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.800
The study was reported on Science Daily.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.640
A research team in Sicily flew drones into the plume

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.320
above an active volcano to sample gases near the crater rim

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.340
using laser spectroscopy.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.200
The approach aims to detect changes in gas composition

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.580
that precede eruptions.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.980
Early tests show the drones can maintain position

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.240
in turbulent air while collecting data

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.100
that ground instruments cannot reach.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.320
The work was reported on Fizz.org.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.660
Researchers intend the method to reach locations

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.260
inaccessible to fixed monitoring stations.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.880
The project focuses on real-time plume chemistry

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.740
rather than visual or seismic signals alone.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.060
Further flights are planned to refine gas ratio thresholds

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.260
that might indicate impending activity.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:43.440
University of Guttenking trials found that cattle respond

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.100
to virtual boundaries with the same avoidance behavior observed

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.780
at conventional electric fences.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.720
The study measured turning angles and grazing distribution

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.220
across multiple herds.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.200
The data suggest virtual systems can deliver

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.520
comparable spatial control without physical infrastructure.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.820
Animals maintained consistent distance patterns

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.400
whether the boundary was physical or projected.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.640
No additional training beyond initial exposure

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.920
was required for the observed responses.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.540
The approach eliminates the need for wire installation

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.800
or maintenance across large pastures.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.880
University of Potstam researchers showed

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.200
that standard extinction risk models perform differently

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.480
depending on whether climate change removes a species habitat

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.280
or merely relocates it.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.580
Current assessment methods do not distinguish these scenarios

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.400
leading to systematic under or overestimation of risk.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.120
The team calls for revised protocols

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.140
that incorporate habitat dynamics.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.040
Models that treat all habitat change as outright loss

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.760
produced higher risk estimates

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.760
than those allowing for relocation.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.700
The distinction matters most for species

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.860
whose ranges are projected to shift rather than shrink.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.220
Revised frameworks would require separate parameters

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.700
for each type of change.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.800
From cardiac tissue changes driven by a signaling molecule,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.520
we move to another protein that moves toxic material

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.960
between cells in the brain.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.540
Yale researchers identified a pair of neuron surface proteins

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.500
that facilitate transmission of the toxic protein

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.640
associated with Parkinson's disease.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.620
Blocking these proteins in mouse models

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.080
sharply reduced spread of the aggregates

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.700
and slowed disease progression.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.680
The finding supplies a new molecular target for therapies

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.680
aimed at interrupting propagation

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.540
rather than only clearing existing aggregates.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.400
Yale's identification of two neuron surface proteins

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.960
that ferry toxic aggregates between cells

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.240
offers a concrete handle on Parkinson's propagation.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.320
Blocking the proteins in mice cut spread

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.960
and slowed motor decline.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.520
Shifting attention from clearance of existing aggregates

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.860
to interruption of cell-to-cell transfer.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.220
The next step is to determine whether the same surface

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.300
proteins operate in human neurons

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.060
and whether safe inhibitors can be developed.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.320
What remains unclear is how early in the disease

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.100
this transmission begins and whether blocking it

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.020
would still help after symptoms appear.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.960
Lund University surveys using trained detection dogs

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.240
counted living and dead bumblebees

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.280
along 60 Swedish roadverges.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.020
Virges with dense flowers attracted foraging bees

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.680
yet mortality rose sharply where traffic volume was highest.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.660
The results quantify how roadside habitat can function

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.220
as an ecological trap.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.900
Mortality counts were obtained through systematic searches

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.600
aided by the detection dogs.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.700
Highflower verges showed the largest gap

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.540
between attraction and survival.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.360
The findings apply specifically to stretches

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.460
carrying heavy daily vehicle loads.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.700
Low dose exposure to sulfox Lafore changed activity

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.900
of genes involved in reproduction in bumblebee tissues.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.640
The study links the next generation insecticide

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.840
to molecular changes that could affect colony viability

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:03.560
over time.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.780
Because pollinators support roughly one third

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.260
of global food production, the findings

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.500
add to concerns about sublethal pesticide effects.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.200
The work was reported on science daily.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.960
Gene expression shifts were measured in multiple tissue types

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.440
after controlled exposure.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.720
The doses used were below levels that cause immediate mortality.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.400
The results point to longer term effects

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.000
on queen production and colony persistence.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.380
While protein surfaces control disease spread,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.920
other surfaces, those of gold atoms,

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.660
actively reorganized to control chemistry.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.420
Researchers mapped how surface atoms on gold spontaneously form

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.580
protective patterns that prevent oxygen

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:46.760
from reaching the metal.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.200
The reorganization suppresses oxidation far more effectively

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:52.540
than previously modeled.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.700
The mechanism explains the long-term brightness

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.160
of gold objects and points to new ways

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.660
to design gold catalysts for manufacturing

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.660
and clean energy applications.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.680
Most people picture gold as a passive,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.020
chemically inert metal that simply refuses to react.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.900
In reality, atoms on its surface actively rearrange

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.180
into ordered patterns that physically exclude oxygen,

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.800
cutting oxidation rates by as much as a trillion times.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.260
Right now, any gold ring or watch you wear

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:26.660
is protected by this dynamic, atomic reorganization

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.840
rather than by absolute inertness.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.480
The same surface behavior that keeps

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.220
jewelry bright for generations also explains

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.420
why gold remains an effective catalyst

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.040
in industrial processes.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.320
One memorable detail is that the protective reorganization

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.600
suppresses oxidation far beyond

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.400
what static models had predicted.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.260
The practical takeaway is that future catalyst design

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.080
can deliberately exploit surface atom mobility

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.680
instead of treating gold as a fixed, unreactive platform.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:06:59.800
Engineers developed a robotic sensor

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:03.600
that distinguishes gram-positive from gram-negative bacteria

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.420
by surface mechanical properties alone.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.640
The system bypasses chemical staining

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.000
and trained interpretation steps required

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.820
in conventional gram tests.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.520
It is intended for rapid screening

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:19.120
in food safety, environmental monitoring,

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:20.580
and infection control.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.320
Classification occurs through direct physical contact

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.900
without added reagents.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.840
The sensor provides results in a single automated pass.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.400
Developers aim to integrate the device

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.180
into portable field units.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.560
From atomic scale self-organization and metals,

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.220
we turn to engineered self-organization

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:41.640
inside living cells.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:45.900
Post-ec engineers rebuilt the ribosomes protein synthesis

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.880
machinery into an RNA-based platform

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:51.720
that reads up to six intracellular signals

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.400
and autonomously triggers a program response.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.600
The circuit-let-cells act as simple decision-making units

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.020
without external control.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.180
The work moves synthetic biology closer

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.140
to autonomous cellular computers.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.340
The study was reported on Fizz.org.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:12.100
A genetic screen in SacramiCe's CeraVCA

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:16.780
showed that no single PIF-1 or RecQ family helicase

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:20.480
is essential for tolerance to the G-4 stabilizing compound

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:21.580
periodostatin.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.980
Sensitivity appeared only in double mutants

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.840
spanning both families and an additional helicase,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.440
SRS-2, proved non-redundant for managing recombination.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:36.440
The results support a two-layer model of G-4 maintenance.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.800
The work was reported on Bio-RXI.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.620
Single-helicase deletions left cells

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.060
able to grow in the presence of the compound.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.920
Only combined loss of representatives

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.620
from both families produced clear sensitivity.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.780
SRS-2 function remained necessary

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.100
even when other helicases were intact.

00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:00.180
Reliable cellular decision-making depends on redundancy.

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.900
Next, we look at how neural circuits

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:04.640
achieve the same robustness.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.160
University of Hong Kong researchers

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.960
working with Princeton and Columbia colleagues

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.220
mapped sensory motor circuits in C elegans

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.020
that maintain function even when individual genes

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.500
or connections are removed.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.100
The study demonstrates how backup pathways compensate

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.200
for disruptions.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.340
The findings help explain why some neural defects

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.960
produce little observable change in behavior.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.560
From biological redundancy that preserves function,

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.020
we examine a material's failure mode

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:35.840
where redundancy is absent.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.320
Researchers traced how soft lithium filaments

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.820
fracture the rigid ceramic electrolyte,

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.860
creating short circuit pathways.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:46.940
The work supplies a mechanistic explanation

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:48.980
for a long-standing failure mode.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.180
Solving the cracking problem could enable safer,

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.220
higher energy solid state cells for vehicles and electronics.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.240
Experiments revealed cracks propagating

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.180
through non-elastic simple fluids,

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.220
challenging the assumption that only complex elastic fluids

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:05.760
can break.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.780
The observation forces a re-examination

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:11.320
of the physical conditions required for fracture.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.580
The work may affect models of material failure

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.860
in industrial and biological settings.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.880
The study was reported in Quanta magazine.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.160
Cracks formed and advanced under controlled shear

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.460
without measurable elastic recovery.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.800
The fluids tested lacked the long-chain polymers

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.080
previously thought necessary for fracture.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.280
The results indicate that fracture criteria

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:36.560
must be broadened beyond elasticity.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.220
New geological mapping suggests

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:42.660
etna is sustained by magma rising through fractures

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.760
created by tectonic plate motion instead of fitting

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:48.840
any of the three classic volcanic formation models.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:53.800
Confirmation would place etna in a rare fourth category

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.420
and imply that large volcanoes can arise

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.460
through mechanisms previously linked

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:00.700
only to small submarine eruptions.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.960
Mapping focused on deep structural features beneath the volcano.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.680
The proposed pathway does not rely

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.580
on sub-deduction-related melting.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:13.440
The findings reopen questions about how other large

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.140
volcanic systems are supplied.

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:19.100
From failure mechanisms at the atomic and materials level,

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:22.860
we close with a broader look at how small molecular details

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.440
scale to visible outcomes.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.760
Before we go, keep an eye on follow-up work from the Yale team

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:32.500
on whether the same surface proteins operate in human neurons.

